<StatePage title="Notice of Intent to Lien: Required Notice or Payment Demand?" description="Distinguish a statutory notice of intent from an optional collection letter. Plan Colorado notice lead time and avoid confusing preliminary notices with filing." canonical="https://mechanicslienform.com/notice-of-intent-to-lien/"><article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 prose prose-lg"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/mechanics-lien/">Document guides</a></nav>
<h1>Notice of intent to lien: identify the purpose before sending</h1><p>A notice of intent may be a required statutory step or an optional collection letter, depending on the state and claim. A document's title does not establish its legal effect. Begin with the property state, contract tier, type of project, and remaining filing period.</p>
<h2>Colorado requires notice before recording</h2><p>Under Colorado section 38-22-109(3), notice of intent must precede lien-statement filing by at least ten days. The statute identifies recipients and service methods and requires service proof with the filed statement. Review the <a href="/mechanics-lien/colorado/">Colorado guide</a> before scheduling recording.</p><p>Work backward from the verified filing limit to allow proper service and lead time. An optional demand email does not automatically satisfy statutory contents, recipients, method, or proof requirements. Resolve uncertain recipient details while enough time remains to complete the prescribed process.</p>
<h2>Early preliminary notices are different</h2><p>The <a href="/notice-to-owner/florida/">Florida Notice to Owner</a> and <a href="/preliminary-notice/california/">California preliminary notice</a> concern early furnishing and preservation of rights. They should not be postponed until a payment dispute. Likewise, Georgia's Notice to Contractor and Washington's preclaim notice are not universal thirty-day or ten-day notices of intent before recording.</p><p><a href="/mechanics-lien/illinois/">Illinois subcontractor notices</a> and <a href="/mechanics-lien/north-carolina/">North Carolina lien-agent and funds notices</a> have their own functions. Identify those tasks separately from the collection letter in the project file. A generic letter cannot be presumed to replace them.</p>
<h2>For an optional payment demand, make the facts easy to review</h2><p>Organize the claimant, customer, project, unpaid invoices, credits, work description, and requested response. Attach or identify the relevant invoice schedule. Keep the amount consistent with the ledger and avoid claiming fees or penalties whose contractual or legal basis has not been established.</p><ol><li>Name the project and party responsible for payment.</li><li>Describe the unpaid work and invoice balance accurately.</li><li>State a realistic response date that leaves the legal filing period intact.</li><li>Provide a contact method for resolving discrepancies.</li><li>Preserve the sent letter, attachments, and delivery evidence.</li></ol><p>Do not imply that recording guarantees payment or prevents every sale. A lien can be challenged, discharged, or transferred to security. Choose accurate wording about contemplated action and have counsel review disputed entitlement or amounts.</p>
<h2>Track collection and legal tasks separately</h2><p>A promised payment date does not necessarily suspend recording or enforcement deadlines. Assign separate reminders for statutory notice, lien filing, service, and litigation. If the requested response date conflicts with a legal task, preserve the legal task rather than letting the collection process silently consume the remaining period.</p><p>Keep an incoming-response log with dates, names, documents, and payment proposals. If payment is disputed, save the full response with the contract and furnishing records. This makes attorney review more efficient than a summary based on recollection.</p>
<h2>Prepare the next document only when appropriate</h2><p>If the claim proceeds, use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">claim preparation checklist</a> and the correct <a href="/mechanics-lien/">state guide</a>. <a href="/mechanics-lien/new-york/">New York</a>, for example, has separate service-proof filing deadlines that a collection letter does not address.</p><p>If payment resolves the dispute, review a <a href="/lien-waiver/conditional/">conditional waiver</a> and, for an already filed claim, the <a href="/mechanics-lien/release/">discharge checklist</a>. Match the document's scope to the settlement and preserve cleared-payment evidence.</p><h2>Official source</h2><p><a href="https://olls.info/crs/crs2025-title-38.htm">Colorado Revised Statutes, Title 38, section 38-22-109</a> provides the statutory notice requirements discussed here. This page is general information and does not establish that an optional demand preserves lien rights.</p></article></StatePage>